          delivery, warranty and risk are counted, gives the tactics for each, and marks the floor
          below which a cheaper machine is no longer a saving.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Where Is the Cheapest Place to Buy a Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The cheapest place to buy a decent treadmill is usually a private used sale of a
            full-size machine from an established brand, followed by model-year clearance and
            manufacturer-refurbished units. The cheapest new treadmills on Amazon and Walmart are
            cheap to buy but are mostly walking pads, and they are only the cheapest option if
            walking is all you will ever do.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            That ranking assumes two things: that you can inspect a machine properly, and that you
            can move a 200-to-300-pound object. If you cannot do either, the order changes, and the
            delivered, warrantied channels move up. The table below ranks the main options by the
            real cost of a machine you would be happy to jog on, with the trade each one makes.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Rank and where</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typical price</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Delivery</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Warranty</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Risk</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1. Private used sale (Marketplace, Craigslist, OfferUp, Nextdoor)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Often a third to half of the original price for a machine a few years old</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">You collect it</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">High; your inspection is the only protection</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2. Model-year clearance (maker direct, specialty dealers)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commonly 20–35% below the usual price</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Usually available, sometimes included</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Normally the full new term</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Low</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3. Manufacturer-refurbished or outlet</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commonly 15–30% below new</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Usually shipped</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Often shorter than new; varies</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Low to medium</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4. Used-gear stores (e.g. Play It Again Sports)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Between private used and refurbished</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sometimes, for a fee</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Store policy, often short</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Medium</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5. Open-box and resale programmes (Amazon Resale, Walmart Restored, Best Buy open-box)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Varies by grade</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Shipped or store pickup</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Depends on grade and seller</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Medium</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6. Warehouse clubs (Costco, Sam&rsquo;s Club)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Competitive delivered prices on a small, rotating range</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Often included online</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Full new term; returns widely regarded as generous</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Low</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7. Budget new listings on Amazon and Walmart</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The lowest new prices</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Shipped</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Brand-dependent, often short</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Medium; specifications are unreliable</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8. Gym closeouts and surplus auctions (GovDeals, liquidators)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Can be very low</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">You remove it, often within days</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None; sold as-is</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">High, plus power and weight problems</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Holiday sales are not on the list because they are a <em>when</em>, not a <em>where</em>:
            Black Friday, the model-year changeover and the spring and summer lull apply to ranks 2,
            3, 6 and 7 and are covered in their own section below. The price ranges are typical
            patterns, not quotes — the clearance and refurbished bands are the ones our
            <a href="/treadmills-for-sale/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for sale guide</a>
            uses, and the used range is the depreciation pattern our
            <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill guide</a>
            describes. Those two pages explain the markets themselves. This one is about getting the
            lowest real cost out of each.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="real-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. How to Work Out the Real Cost of a Cheap Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Every cheap channel shifts some cost off the sticker and onto you. A fair comparison puts
            it back. The arithmetic is simple enough to do on a phone in a stranger's garage:
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Real cost per year = (price + getting it home + assembly + fees + the repairs you
            should expect − what you could sell it for) ÷ the years you will use it.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            None of those inputs is knowable exactly, so pick honest guesses and keep them the same
            across the machines you compare. Here is the calculation for four common routes. Every
            figure is an <em>assumption chosen to show the method</em>, not a price we found or a
            lifespan anyone has measured; swap in your own numbers.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A new walking pad at $230,</strong> delivered free, assumed to last three years of daily walking and to be worth nothing at the end: $230 ÷ 3 ≈ <strong>$77 a year</strong>. It walks; it does not run.</li>
            <li><strong>A used full-size folding treadmill from an established brand at $400,</strong> plus an assumed $120 for a van and a helper and $40 for silicone lubricant and a safety key, assumed to last six more years and to resell for $150: ($400 + $120 + $40 − $150) ÷ 6 ≈ <strong>$68 a year</strong>, on a machine with a deck you can jog on.</li>
            <li><strong>A manufacturer-refurbished machine at $900</strong> with delivery included and a warranty, assumed to last eight years and to resell for $200: ($900 − $200) ÷ 8 ≈ <strong>$88 a year</strong>, with the least risk of the four.</li>
            <li><strong>An ex-gym commercial treadmill won at auction for $500,</strong> plus an assumed 15% buyer's premium ($75), $250 for heavy-item transport and $350 for an electrician to add a dedicated circuit, assumed to last ten years and resell for $300: ($500 + $75 + $250 + $350 − $300) ÷ 10 ≈ <strong>$88 a year</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Two things fall out of that. First, the gap between the cheapest sticker and the most
            expensive one is $670, but the gap in yearly cost is about $20. Second, the ranking is
            fragile: if the used machine needs a $250 motor controller in year two, or the walking
            pad fails at eighteen months, the order changes. That is why risk sits next to price in
            the table above. A cheap channel is only cheap if the machine works for the years you
            assumed.
          </p>
          <p>
            The biggest single swing is use. A treadmill you stop using after three months costs the
            same whether it was cheap or not, and only its resale value comes back. If you are not sure
            you will use one, the cheapest treadmill of all is the one you do not buy yet — our
            <a href="/home-treadmill-vs-gym-membership/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs gym membership</a>
            comparison has cheaper ways to test the habit first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-marketplaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Used Marketplaces: The Cheapest Hardware Per Dollar</h2>
<p>
            Private sales on Facebook Marketplace, Craigslist, OfferUp and Nextdoor are where a
            full-size machine gets cheapest, because a treadmill is heavy, awkward and resented by the
            person whose spare room it fills. Treadmills tend to lose much of their value in the first
            few years and then flatten, so — as arithmetic on that pattern — a machine that was
            $1,500 new might be listed at around $750 at two years old and closer to $500 at three to
            five. Sellers who are moving house or clearing an estate often go lower to make it
            disappear. Our
            <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to where used treadmills turn up</a>
            covers the sources, safe meetings and scams; here are the tactics for getting the price
            down without buying a problem.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to search for</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Brand names, not just "treadmill".</strong> Search for the established home brands — Sole, Horizon, NordicTrack, ProForm, Precor, Life Fitness, Landice — and model families you have already shortlisted. A name search filters out the listings for walking pads and toys that swamp a generic search.</li>
            <li><strong>The words sellers use when they want it gone.</strong> "Must go", "moving", "you haul", "free to whoever picks up", "basement" and "estate sale" find motivated sellers. So do misspellings such as "tredmill" and "treadmil", which fewer buyers see.</li>
            <li><strong>Other names for the same thing.</strong> "Running machine", "exercise equipment" and "home gym" catch listings that never use the word treadmill.</li>
            <li><strong>A sensible price window and a wide radius.</strong> Set an upper limit that matches a decent machine, not your hopes, and widen the search distance: a cheap machine an hour away is often a better buy than an expensive one down the road, provided you can transport it.</li>
            <li><strong>Saved searches with alerts.</strong> The good listings go quickly. Supply also follows the calendar, with more machines appearing once the new-year resolutions fade, which our <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">timing guide</a> covers.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to ask before you travel</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The model number and the serial number,</strong> from the label on the frame. Look the model up for its motor rating, deck size and original price, and check it against the recall database. A seller who will not give you the model is telling you something.</li>
            <li><strong>How old it is and how it was used.</strong> One walker three times a week is a different history from a family of runners.</li>
            <li><strong>Whether the belt has ever been lubricated or replaced,</strong> and whether it has thrown any error codes.</li>
            <li><strong>A short video of it running at jogging speed with someone on it,</strong> with the sound on. It is not an inspection, but it saves wasted trips.</li>
            <li><strong>Whether it folds, how it is held up, and whether the seller will help you load it.</strong> Ask where it is in the house: a machine in a basement up narrow stairs is a bigger job than one in a garage.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Red flags that cancel the saving</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A price far below everything else for the same model, especially with a story about being away and shipping it to you. Never pay a deposit for a machine you have not seen.</li>
            <li>A machine that is "running fine" but already unplugged, disassembled or wrapped for collection when you arrive, so you cannot test it.</li>
            <li>A belt that hesitates or surges under your weight, a burning smell, or a motor hood that is hot after a few minutes at moderate speed.</li>
            <li>A walking pad or very small motorised machine more than a couple of years old. These are the machines where a used saving makes least sense, because the motor and controller are most of the value.</li>
            <li>An unbranded marketplace machine with no model number you can find anywhere. You cannot check its specification, its parts supply or its recall status.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            When you see it, run our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill buying checklist</a>
            — it takes about fifteen minutes and is the only warranty a private sale has. Then
            negotiate on what you found, not on a general wish for a lower price: a dry belt, a frayed
            belt edge or a <a href="/treadmill-safety-key-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>missing safety key</a> are concrete reasons a seller will accept.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clearance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Model-Year Clearance and Floor Models</h2>
<p>
            For a new machine with a full warranty, outgoing-model clearance is the best value in the
            category. Treadmill model-year refreshes tend to change the console and software far more
            than the frame, motor and deck, so last year's version of a solid machine is usually
            nearly the same treadmill for noticeably less. Our
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">price-cycle guide</a>
            explains when the changeover tends to happen.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to find it</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Look for a new version on the maker's site.</strong> When a maker lists a new model in a family, the previous one is often still sold — by the maker, by Amazon sellers and by dealers — at a lower price. Search for the old model name specifically.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask specialty fitness dealers about floor models and last year's stock.</strong> Showrooms need the floor space when new models arrive. A floor model has had people stand on it, not train on it, and dealers will often include delivery or assembly to move it.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the "outlet", "clearance" or "previous model" sections</strong> on maker and retailer sites rather than waiting for a sale banner.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to ask</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Is the warranty the same as the current model's, and does it start from my purchase date?</li>
            <li>Is this new in the box, a floor model, or a return? The three are priced and warranted differently.</li>
            <li>What actually changed in the new model? If the answer is the screen and the app, the old one is probably the better buy.</li>
            <li>Are parts and consoles still supported? For any established brand they normally are for years, but ask.</li>
            <li>Does any included app subscription still come with it, and do you want it? Our <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmills without subscriptions</a> covers what a console needs to work alone.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The catch is selection. Clearance means whatever is left, in whatever quantity, and the
            machine you want may sell out. Have your specification decided — deck length, continuous
            motor rating, user weight — so you can recognise the right deal when it appears instead
            of buying the wrong machine because it is reduced.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="open-box-refurbished" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Open-Box, Resale and Refurbished: Cheaper New-ish Machines</h2>
<p>
            Between a private sale and a new machine sits a group of channels that resell returned,
            damaged-box or reconditioned treadmills with some protection attached. The discount is
            usually smaller than a private sale; what you are paying for is the return path and, in
            the better programmes, a warranty. The single question that decides whether the deal is
            good is <strong>who stands behind the machine, and for how long</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Amazon Resale.</strong> Amazon renamed its Amazon Warehouse store Amazon Resale in
            2024. It sells used, pre-owned and open-box items graded Like New, Very Good, Good or
            Acceptable, and reporting at the time said purchases carry the same delivery and return
            policies as other Amazon orders. For a treadmill, read the condition notes closely —
            "missing hardware" or "damaged packaging" matter more on a 150-pound box than on a phone —
            and check whether the manufacturer's warranty applies to a resale unit. Our guide to
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a>
            covers the seller questions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walmart Restored.</strong> Walmart's restored listings are graded too. Its product
            pages described the Premium tier as carrying a minimum one-year warranty, and the Like New,
            Good and Fair tiers as carrying a 90-day free return, when we checked. Many restored
            listings are sold by third-party sellers, whose warranty terms can differ, and Walmart
            tells shoppers to ask the seller for the warranty terms before buying. Large items can also
            carry their own return conditions, so read the listing rather than the programme page.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Best Buy open-box and other big-box returns.</strong> Open-box treadmills appear at
            big-box retailers, sometimes only at a particular store. Our
            <a href="/best-buy-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Best Buy treadmill guide</a>
            covers how that retailer's listings work.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Manufacturer-refurbished and outlet units.</strong> These are the safest of the
            group, because the company that built the machine has inspected it and usually warrants
            it, though often for less than a new one. Our
            <a href="/refurbished-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">refurbished treadmills guide</a>
            explains the three different things sold as "refurbished" and what should have been
            replaced; it is worth reading before you compare a refurbished price with a used one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two tactics apply to the whole group. Compare the open-box price with the current new
            price, not the list price — an open-box unit discounted from an inflated "was" price can
            cost more than the same machine new on sale. And check how a return would physically work
            before you buy: the shipping, the collection and whether the box is needed. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">return policies guide</a>
            sets out what to look for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clubs-and-budget-listings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Warehouse Clubs and Budget Listings on Amazon and Walmart</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Warehouse clubs</strong> earn their place for one reason: they often quote a
            delivered price and back it with returns widely regarded as generous. Their range is small
            and rotates, and many models carry club-specific model numbers, so compare the
            specification line by line with the nearest mainstream model. If you are not already a
            member, add the membership fee to the price. Our guide to
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-at-costco/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill at Costco</a>
            covers how to judge a club-exclusive model.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The cheapest new listings on Amazon and Walmart</strong> are where most searches for
            a cheap treadmill end, and where the most money is wasted. At the lowest prices you are
            almost always buying a walking pad or a very light folding machine, frequently from a
            marketplace brand, and the listing's motor horsepower, speed and weight capacity can
            disagree with the manual. That does not make them bad buys. It makes them buys for a
            specific job. Four tactics keep them honest:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Read the belt size, not the headline.</strong> A belt shorter than about 50 inches is a walking surface, whatever the listing says about running.</li>
            <li><strong>Ignore peak horsepower.</strong> Look for a continuous rating, and treat a missing one as a low one. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a> explains the difference.</li>
            <li><strong>Check who the seller is.</strong> A machine sold and shipped by the retailer has a clearer return path than one from a marketplace seller you cannot reach.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the price history.</strong> Budget listings run near-permanent "deals" against list prices few people ever paid. A price-history tracker shows what the machine has really sold for.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-300/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $300</a>
            and
            <a href="/best-budget-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best budget treadmills</a>
            roundups name the machines whose figures held up when we checked them, which saves you
            reading forty near-identical listings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gym-closeouts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Gym Closeouts, Surplus Auctions and Ex-Commercial Machines</h2>
<p>
            Gyms replace their cardio on a schedule, which keeps a steady supply of retired commercial
            treadmills moving through the used market. Planet Fitness, for one, says in its annual
            report for 2025 that its franchisees generally replace club equipment every five to nine
            years, depending on club volume. Universities, hotels, apartment buildings, military
            bases and city recreation centres retire equipment too. Those machines were built for
            many hours a day, and a working one can be a remarkable bargain — or a 400-pound mistake.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where they turn up</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Public surplus auctions.</strong> Government and university surplus offices sell retired equipment through platforms such as GovDeals. UC San Diego's surplus office, for example, lists retired treadmills there and sells as-is, where-is, with all sales final once the item is removed.</li>
            <li><strong>Liquidation auctions</strong> when a gym closes or changes hands, often advertised locally and run over a few days.</li>
            <li><strong>Remanufacturers and used commercial dealers,</strong> who buy club equipment in bulk, recondition it and sell it with a short warranty. Dearer than an auction, far safer.</li>
            <li><strong>Direct from a gym that is re-equipping.</strong> Asking an independent gym's manager whether they sell retiring machines costs nothing.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What makes an auction bargain expensive</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Fees.</strong> Many auction platforms and sellers add a buyer's premium, tax and sometimes a handling charge on top of the winning bid. Read the terms of the lot before you bid.</li>
            <li><strong>Removal.</strong> Lots usually have to be collected within a short window, by you, from a loading dock or a basement. A commercial treadmill commonly weighs around 400 pounds; Life Fitness lists its <a href="/life-fitness-integrity-series-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Integrity treadmill</a> at 425 to 434 pounds.</li>
            <li><strong>Power.</strong> Commercial treadmills generally need a dedicated circuit, and some need more than a household outlet supplies. Life Fitness specifies a dedicated 120-volt, 20-amp circuit for the Integrity, and some commercial machines need 208 or 240 volts. Find the electrical requirement for the exact model before you bid.</li>
            <li><strong>No testing.</strong> Surplus machines are often sold without being run, sometimes with the console missing.</li>
            <li><strong>Recalls.</strong> Johnson Health Tech has recalled Matrix T1 and T3 series commercial treadmills, in 2022 and again in an expanded recall in 2024, because the power cord can work loose from the socket and pose a fire hazard. The remedy is a power cord bracket. Check any ex-gym Matrix against the recall notices before you buy.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our guide to putting a
            <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym treadmill in your home</a>
            covers the floor, the stairs and the circuit in detail, and
            <a href="/what-treadmills-do-gyms-use/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what treadmills gyms use</a>
            tells you which brands you are likely to find and what makes them different. If any of
            those three questions has no good answer, a remanufactured unit with delivery included, or
            a home machine, will be cheaper in the end.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sales" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Holiday Sales and Timing: When Cheap Gets Cheaper</h2>
<p>
            Timing does not create new channels, but it moves prices within them, and in this category
            the swing over a year is large. Three patterns matter for a buyer on a tight budget.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The late-November sales are real for mid-range machines and murky for cheap
            ones.</strong> Established brands discount genuinely around Black Friday and Cyber Monday.
            Budget listings run "deals" all year against list prices that are rarely paid, so the
            November figure may be no lower than October's. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday treadmill guide</a>
            explains how to tell a real reduction from a presentational one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>January is the worst month to buy new and a decent month to start watching
            used.</strong> Demand peaks with resolutions, so new prices are firm. A few weeks later the
            same resolutions start to fade and used listings rise, through late winter and spring.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The model-year changeover is the quiet best deal.</strong> Clearance of outgoing
            stock produces smaller headline percentages than Black Friday and bigger real savings,
            with much less fanfare. Retailer holiday weekends through the year — Presidents Day,
            Memorial Day, Labor Day — and Amazon's summer Prime Day event also bring promotions, but
            judge each against a price you recorded yourself.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical habit is the same in every case: write down the delivered price of the two or
            three machines on your shortlist, with the date, and check it again when a sale starts. A
            reduction you can measure is a saving. A reduction from a "was" price you have never seen is
            marketing. The full month-by-month cycle is in our
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best time of year to buy a treadmill</a>
            guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-floor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. The Floor: When a Cheap Treadmill Stops Being a Saving</h2>
<p>
            There is a price below which a cheaper treadmill does not save money, because it buys a
            different and smaller product. For a new machine in 2026, that line sits at roughly $300.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Under about $300 new, you are buying a walking pad.</strong> When we checked the
            machines in our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-300/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$300 roundup</a>,
            every one we could verify at that price was a flat deck with a motor and a remote, some
            with a fold-up bar. Belts ran from about 35 to 42.5 inches long, and where the motor's real
            continuous rating could be found it came out well under the headline figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What a sub-$300 machine can do:</strong> daily walking at a steady pace, walking
            while working at a standing desk, and a few minutes of light jogging on the 2-in-1 models
            for a lighter user. For that job it is often excellent value, and a good walking pad is a
            better walking machine than a stretched folding treadmill at the same price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it cannot do:</strong> sustained running, which needs a deck of roughly 55 to
            60 inches; regular use near its weight limit; meaningful incline on most models; and
            years of heavy use. A machine bought for a job it cannot do is the most expensive cheap
            treadmill there is, because you buy it and then buy the right one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The next step up is not far away.</strong> Our under-$300 guide found folding
            treadmills with a 55-inch belt and powered incline from roughly $420 on sale in 2026, and
            our <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$500 guide</a>
            covers that band. If you want to jog and your budget is near $300, the honest options are
            to save to that level, or to spend the same $300 on a used full-size machine from an
            established brand and inspect it properly. Our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new vs used comparison</a>
            works through that choice.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you do buy at the floor, protect the purchase.</strong> Most cheap machines fail
            for avoidable reasons — a dry belt, a heavier user than the rating, long sessions with no
            rest, a dusty room. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-make-a-cheap-treadmill-last-longer/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">making a cheap treadmill last longer</a>
            covers the habits that stretch its life, and every extra year it lasts lowers the real cost
            you worked out above.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Before You Pay: A Cheap-Treadmill Checklist</h2>
<p>
            Whichever channel you use, run through these before money changes hands.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Is it the right machine for the job?</strong> Belt length for your use, a continuous motor rating, and a user-weight rating comfortably above your weight.</li>
            <li><strong>What is the real cost per year?</strong> Price plus transport, assembly and fees, minus resale, divided by realistic years of use.</li>
            <li><strong>Who stands behind it?</strong> The manufacturer, the retailer, a reseller or nobody — and for how long, in writing.</li>
            <li><strong>How would a return physically work?</strong> Collection, shipping and whether you need the box.</li>
            <li><strong>Has it been recalled, or warned about?</strong> Search the model on the CPSC's recalls and its product safety warnings. Recent examples that matter for second-hand buyers: Peloton recalled the Tread+ and the Tread in May 2021; Johnson Health Tech expanded its Matrix power-cord recall in January 2026 to some Matrix and Vision home treadmills made between 2007 and 2024; and on 16 April 2026 the CPSC warned owners to stop using four Sperax walking pad and treadmill models immediately and not to resell them, after the importer declined to recall them. A used Sperax is not a bargain at any price.</li>
            <li><strong>Can you get it home and up the stairs?</strong> Measure the route, check the boxed or assembled weight, and line up help. Our <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">moving guide</a> covers the hard parts.</li>
            <li><strong>Will it plug in?</strong> Home machines need a properly grounded outlet and often recommend a dedicated circuit; commercial machines usually require one.</li>
            <li><strong>Is the price lower than it was last month?</strong> Compare with a price you recorded, not with a "was" price.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are unsure how much machine you need in the first place, start with
            <a href="/how-much-should-you-spend-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much to spend on a treadmill</a>,
            then come back here to find the cheapest way to buy it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where is the cheapest place to buy a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a decent full-size machine, usually a private used sale from an established brand, found on Facebook Marketplace, Craigslist, OfferUp or Nextdoor, followed by model-year clearance and manufacturer-refurbished units. The cheapest new treadmills on Amazon and Walmart cost least at checkout but are mostly walking pads, so they are only the cheapest option if you will only walk.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I buy a treadmill for cheap near me?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with local marketplace listings and used-gear stores such as Play It Again Sports, then ask specialty fitness dealers about floor models and last year's stock. Public surplus auctions, such as university and city listings on GovDeals, sometimes include retired gym treadmills, but those are sold as-is and you must remove them yourself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth buying a cheap treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it when the machine matches the job. A walking pad bought for walking is good value. The same pad bought for running is not, because its belt is short and its motor is rated for walking, and you end up buying a second machine. Work out the cost per year, including delivery, repairs and resale, rather than comparing sticker prices.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should a decent cheap treadmill cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                New, a walking pad costs roughly $150 to $300, and a folding treadmill with a 55-inch belt and powered incline started at roughly $420 on sale when we checked in October 2026. Used, a full-size machine from an established brand often sells for a third to half of its original price after a few years. These are typical ranges, not quotes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are open-box and refurbished treadmills worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, if you know who stands behind the machine. Manufacturer-refurbished units are the safest because the maker inspected them and usually warrants them, though often for less time than new. Retail open-box and resale programmes vary by condition grade and seller, so check the warranty and the return terms on the listing and compare the price with the current new price, not the list price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I buy a used gym treadmill cheaply?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Gyms, universities and hotels retire treadmills on a cycle, and they appear at surplus auctions, liquidation sales and remanufacturers. Auction prices can be very low, but add the buyer's premium, heavy-item transport and any electrical work, since commercial machines usually need a dedicated circuit. Check the model against the CPSC recall database before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Buying a Cheap Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most expensive cheap treadmills became expensive in one of four ways, and all four are avoidable before you pay.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing sticker prices instead of real cost</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A $230 walking pad and a $900 refurbished treadmill can cost about the same per year once delivery, lifespan and resale are counted. Do the per-year arithmetic with the same assumptions for every option before you decide which is cheap.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a walking machine for running</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Under about $300 new you are buying a walking pad. Bought for walking, it is good value; bought for running, it is a short belt and a walking motor that you will replace with the machine you needed in the first place.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Winning an auction you cannot collect or power</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Ex-gym treadmills commonly weigh around 400 pounds, must often be removed within days, and usually need a dedicated circuit. Check the lot terms, the transport and the electrical requirement before you bid, not after you win.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the recall check on a used machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Recalled treadmills stay in the used market for years. Search the model on the CPSC recall database before you pay, and if a remedy exists, such as the Matrix power cord bracket, make sure it has been fitted or order it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

